The Connection n1.next=n2 links the first node to the second node.
you have now built the foundation of the linked list.

4. Build a complete Singly Linked List 

Very Important --> Instead of creating and connecting nodes manually, lets create a LinkedList class that manages them.
We will begin with three operations.
1. Insert a node at the end 
2. Display all nodes
3. Count the nodes

Code:

class Node:
      def __init__(self,data):
          self.data=data
          self.next=None
class LinkedList:
      def __init__(self):
          self.head=None
      def append(self,data):
          new_Node=Node(data)
          if self.head is None:
             self.head=new_Node
             return
          current=self.head
          while current.next is not None:
             current=current.next
          current.next=new_Node
      def display(self):
          current=self.head
          while current is not None:
              print("value: ", current.data)
              current=current.next
      def count(self):
          count=0
          current=self.head
          while current is not None:
              count+=1
              current=current.next
          print("count: ", count)
ll=LinkedList()
ll.append(10)
ll.append(20)
ll.append(30)
ll.display()
ll.count()

Output: 

value: 10
value: 20
value: 30
count: 3

How does append() work ? 
Suppose your linked list contains 10->20->None and you want to add 30.
Before Insertion :
10->20->None
After Insertion :
10->20->30->None

the algorithm follows the next reference untill it find the final node, then connects that node to the
new Node.

VERY IMPORTANT LINE: The important line:

current.next=new_Node

It connects the last node to the new Node.

5. Insert elements at different position.
In Linked Lists, insertion can happen at the beginning, end or at a specified position.

A. Insertion at the beginning

Code:

def prepend(self, data):
    current=self.head
    new_Node=Node(data)
    new_Node.next=current
    self.head=new_Node

Add this method inside the linkedList class.
suppose the existing linked list is:
20->30->None
After, ll.append(10) it becomes:
10->20->30->None

VERY IMPORTANT --> The order of these two statements matter:

new_Node.next=current
self.head=new_Node

first connect the new node to the old head.
then make the new node the head.
TIME COMPLEXITY: O(1) because no traversal is required.

